      "side": 1,
      "message": "Understand that this is needed to keep track of current index when parsing the input array and Java does not provide a good way to return both result and index after each parsing step, neither does Java have a standard mutable integer implementation. \n\nHowever, having a static counter may create race conditions in the future. For example, multiple threads might be calling BassData.parseBaseData() at the same time and the sParseOffset value might be modified by these threads. \n\nA simple way to fix this is to add \"synchronized\" to parseBaseData, but this will hurt the code\u0027s performance since we would only be able to parse one BASE array at a time. \n\nHence, after some thinking, a better way might be this:\n\nCreate a helper class\n\n```\nclass BaseInformationWithOffset {\n    private BaseInformation mBaseInformation;\n    private int mParseOffset;\n    \n    BaseInformationWithOffset(BaseInformation baseInformation, int parseOffset) {\n        mBaseInformation \u003d baseInformation;\n        mParseOffset \u003d parseOffset;\n    }\n    \n    BaseInformation getBaseInformation() {\n        return mBaseInformation;\n    }\n    \n    int getParseOffset() {\n        return mParseOffset;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThen change the following method\u0027s signature to accept an offset and return the BaseInformationWithOffset helper class\n\n```\nprivate static BaseInformationWithOffset parseLevelTwo(byte[] serviceData, int groupIndex, int parseOffset) {\n    // Parse the BASE level two information\n    // Change parseOffset in this method as needed\n    // When you are ready to return\n    return new BaseInformationWithOffset(node, parseOffset);\n}\n\nprivate static BaseInformationWithOffset parseLevelThree(byte[] serviceData, int parseOffset) {\n    // Same as above\n    return new BaseInformationWithOffset(node, parseOffset);\n}\n```\n\nThen in `parseBaseData`, you can declare parseOffset as a local variable and update it using the return values from the above two methods\n\n```\nstatic BaseData parseBaseData(byte[] serviceData) {\n    int parseOffset \u003d 0;\n    // do level one parsing\n    // when it is time to do level two parsing\n    BaseInformationWithOffset levelBaseInformationWithOffset \u003d parseLevelTwo(serviceData, groupIndex, parsetOffset);\n    BaseInformation levelTwo \u003d levelBaseInformationWithOffset.getBaseInformation();\n    parseOffset \u003d levelBaseInformationWithOffset.getParseOffset();\n}\n```\n\nUsing this algorithm, you can avoid declaring a globally static sParseOffset variable and the code will be able to parse multiple BASE arrays in parallel. \n\nThanks!",
